530
02:03:02.920 --> 02:03:18.990
Jamie (SG Eco Loop): Enhancing compost utilization. How might SUSS develop sustainable and scalable ways to use or distribute the compost generated on campus, so that it benefits both the SUSS and wider community? So this is more like a… like a distribution challenge statement.

531
02:03:22.630 --> 02:03:36.989
Jamie (SG Eco Loop): That's just more a distribution channel statement. So once you have the output, which is the compost, you know, they use it, all the SUSS, gardens, everything, but maybe that's too much. So what can… who can we pass this excess compost to?

532
02:03:37.090 --> 02:03:41.649
Jamie (SG Eco Loop): Then, term statement 3 is reduction of waste generated by customers.

533
02:03:41.680 --> 02:03:59.139
Jamie (SG Eco Loop): How might SUSS encourage the diners at the selected food outlets, reduce food waste? So maybe order, don't over-order or finish what they have already ordered. So that's the three trend statements from SUSS. Next, we have a chance statement from Vida City. So…

534
02:03:59.320 --> 02:04:08.470
Jamie (SG Eco Loop): They run a few event spaces. Our events generate food and packaging waste almost daily from deliveries, diningware, excess food.

535
02:04:08.510 --> 02:04:27.059
Jamie (SG Eco Loop): Decision makers are often their partners and co-organisers. They like to explore ways to encourage or incentivize partners to adopt reusables, and participants to accept reusables, or a bring your own culture at community events. So how might you build a solution to address this problem?

536
02:04:27.470 --> 02:04:47.030
Jamie (SG Eco Loop): And the third, challenge statement we have is from Kids Tropic, which is an indoor children's playground. So the challenge statement is, how might we design an engaging, play-based learning journey that helps preschool children aged 4 to 6 understand plastic waste and practice simple, sustainable habits in their everyday lives?

537
02:04:47.840 --> 02:04:51.240
Jamie (SG Eco Loop): So, after this, over email, I will be sharing

538
02:04:51.500 --> 02:05:03.320
Jamie (SG Eco Loop): Details about these challenge statements, which is including maybe what will be their implementation considerations, whether they'll be happy to host you if your solution is selected, or not.

539
02:05:03.350 --> 02:05:15.230
Jamie (SG Eco Loop): Alright, so if you are interested, you have to tell me that you want to tackle this challenge statement so that, you know, we can coordinate with the internship partner for the next steps.

540
02:05:15.350 --> 02:05:26.590
Jamie (SG Eco Loop): Alright, optional. Totally optional. You can build a solution for a different problem. You do not have to choose from these, five challenge statements over here.

541
02:05:26.960 --> 02:05:27.750
Jamie (SG Eco Loop): Cliff?

542
02:05:28.190 --> 02:05:29.539
Jamie (SG Eco Loop): Hopefully that's clear.

543
02:05:29.770 --> 02:05:44.230
Jamie (SG Eco Loop): Okay, so we will share more details over email. Any questions, reach out anytime. So instead of a break, we are going to continue, hopefully it's okay, on the last bit on discovery and defining the problem. Skipping this…

544
02:05:44.850 --> 02:05:45.730
Jamie (SG Eco Loop): So…

545
02:05:46.170 --> 02:05:54.940
Jamie (SG Eco Loop): once again, you know, back to… because I know you guys maybe did some desktop research, just now during the 10 minutes. That's very…

546
02:05:54.940 --> 02:06:14.740
Jamie (SG Eco Loop): basic, right? That's very first step, but you need to go back and do a little bit more research about the problem to make sure you understand the context of the entire problem, because you think that this problem is faced by this person, but who else is involved? What else is involved? There's probably no problem that is very, very simple.

547
02:06:14.740 --> 02:06:32.599
Jamie (SG Eco Loop): And if it is, then maybe it's already solved, right? So if it's a problem that still exists, there are probably reasons why it hasn't been solved, and it's probably because, actually, the problem is a lot more complex than you think. There are more people that need to be involved, or people who need to change their mindset, so you need to

548
02:06:32.600 --> 02:06:37.909
Jamie (SG Eco Loop): Go back and do more, research and, kind of interviews or surveys.

549
02:06:37.910 --> 02:06:42.250
Jamie (SG Eco Loop): So, we're gonna go through this kind of three-step process of

550
02:06:42.260 --> 02:06:48.870
Jamie (SG Eco Loop): discovery and defining the problem. So, first thing is to explore, right?

551
02:06:48.940 --> 02:07:00.619
Jamie (SG Eco Loop): And why do you need to do these three steps? It's really to avoid making the wrong assumptions, make sure you're tackling the right problem, and also make sure that you are building the right foundations to build the right solution.

552
02:07:01.350 --> 02:07:07.250
Jamie (SG Eco Loop): Okay? So, I'm sure in your schools, your exams, your teacher always tell you when you're doing your exams.

553
02:07:07.460 --> 02:07:09.489
Jamie (SG Eco Loop): Read the question carefully.

554
02:07:09.940 --> 02:07:19.929
Jamie (SG Eco Loop): I think all of you must have heard this many, many times when you're teachers, because… why? So that when you're answering the question, you're actually answering the right question. It's the same thing here.

555
02:07:19.930 --> 02:07:30.140
Jamie (SG Eco Loop): You need to make sure that whatever you are built, before you decide what to build, right, you need to know what is the problem you are solving for. So, make sure you have a very, very clear understanding of the problem.

556
02:07:31.480 --> 02:07:32.830
Jamie (SG Eco Loop): So, exploring.

557
02:07:33.050 --> 02:07:47.619
Jamie (SG Eco Loop): You should be kind of mapping out everyone that's related to the waste. Is it the individual? Is it the shops? Is it the recycling partner? Is it the government?

558
02:07:47.750 --> 02:07:49.689
Jamie (SG Eco Loop): Is it the regulations?

559
02:07:49.890 --> 02:07:56.289
Jamie (SG Eco Loop): etc. So, mapping out everyone who is related to the creation of the waste.

560
02:07:56.620 --> 02:08:00.650
Jamie (SG Eco Loop): You also should research on what are the solutions that already exist.

561
02:08:01.050 --> 02:08:17.649
Jamie (SG Eco Loop): You don't really want to build something that somebody has already done, it's already fully scaled, and then you are building the same thing, and you are later, you are smaller, doesn't really make a lot of sense. So make sure you already know what's out there when you are building. Make sure you can say why your solution is different from other people's solution.

562
02:08:17.970 --> 02:08:22.400
Jamie (SG Eco Loop): Okay? And then… A journey map of, like, when

563
02:08:22.400 --> 02:08:41.390
Jamie (SG Eco Loop): is the waste created? Is it created, when it's bought, and it's not used, or it's created, before, when they are producing a product, and before it's ready, there's a lot of waste that's created. So, kind of pinpoint when the waste is created. Or is it like, oh, the thing can only be used one time, and after that, it's immediately a waste? Or is it a thing, say, can use

564
02:08:41.390 --> 02:08:48.370
Jamie (SG Eco Loop): 10 times, and afterwards, it's obsolete, and it has to be… it has to find a different, way to use the waste.

565
02:08:49.750 --> 02:08:58.300
Jamie (SG Eco Loop): So, next is looking, so observing. So, what are the activities, environments, interactions, objects, and users?

566
02:08:58.430 --> 02:09:14.040
Jamie (SG Eco Loop): involved in this problem, research on the waste data. So, you need to have a clear baseline before you know, you know, if your solution will make any impact on reducing the waste. So, how much waste is being created from this problem right now?

567
02:09:14.110 --> 02:09:27.419
Jamie (SG Eco Loop): Where is it from? How often is it, created? Is it on a weekly basis? There will be, like, 10 tons of waste that the restaurant throws away, you know, etc. So you need to be very clear on numbers as well.

568
02:09:28.220 --> 02:09:31.429
Jamie (SG Eco Loop): Okay, then the final thing is asking.

569
02:09:31.550 --> 02:09:32.540
Jamie (SG Eco Loop): So…

570
02:09:33.920 --> 02:09:50.099
Jamie (SG Eco Loop): this is where a lot of people will, you know, outsource it to AI, and just, like, you tell me research all about this, and then I take your… whatever the AI tell me as the gospel truth, you know. But that's not enough. Most of the time, you actually need to talk to the real people who face the problems.

571
02:09:50.120 --> 02:09:57.880
Jamie (SG Eco Loop): To be able to know for sure that what you understand about a problem is actually correct, because

572
02:09:58.170 --> 02:10:13.729
Jamie (SG Eco Loop): there's just a lot of, maybe, details that can be lost if you don't talk to the people who really face a problem. It's like… it's like if, you know, your… maybe your teacher or your parent assumes you have certain problems.

573
02:10:13.990 --> 02:10:19.159
Jamie (SG Eco Loop): But actually, they didn't tell you about… you didn't… you didn't have a conversation about it, they just assumed.

574
02:10:19.320 --> 02:10:21.710
Jamie (SG Eco Loop): Do you think that they really know your problems?

575
02:10:22.290 --> 02:10:38.289
Jamie (SG Eco Loop): maybe not, right? It's only when you have a conversation, they actually ask you what is the problem, and you tell them what is the problem, or they give you a safe space to share about your real problems, then they will know, then they will understand. So it's the same thing, you know, if you don't…

576
02:10:38.290 --> 02:10:45.699
Jamie (SG Eco Loop): have a kind of an open conversation with your users, or target users, how will you know the problems they really face? So…

577
02:10:46.440 --> 02:11:01.580
Jamie (SG Eco Loop): when… you don't have to, like, write this down, but these are just questions for you to, to guide your discovery process. So this is called Empathy Map. So you should be, you know, you put a user in the center of the map, and then you are mapping out.

578
02:11:01.680 --> 02:11:05.830
Jamie (SG Eco Loop): What are they thinking and feeling? What are they seeing?

579
02:11:06.090 --> 02:11:24.800
Jamie (SG Eco Loop): Which is, like, the environment around them, what other people are doing, maybe they are very affected by the trends, maybe they will be, you know, if there's a trend to do recycling and it's a trendy thing to do, then they will do recycling. Or what are they saying and doing? So, they can say that I always recycle, but do they actually do it? So.

580
02:11:25.170 --> 02:11:43.450
Jamie (SG Eco Loop): there's a difference between all these things, and then what do they hear? What… what… whether it is, you know, are they, do they take their news from social media? With social media, is it… are they always on TikTok? Are they always on Instagram? What are the type of news that they are hearing, through the channels that they consume?

581
02:11:43.860 --> 02:11:57.520
Jamie (SG Eco Loop): And importantly, so that does this, like, kind of a basic first level of, like, understanding your user. Then, importantly, you need to understand what pains they have, and what gains they need.

582
02:11:57.800 --> 02:11:58.920
Jamie (SG Eco Loop): So…

583
02:12:01.340 --> 02:12:18.110
Jamie (SG Eco Loop): when you are mapping out the pain and the gain, you do need to make sure that the… because this is the first step, right, which is to understand your problem. When you go to the second step, which is building your solution, you need to think back, does my solution actually address a pain that they have? Does it address a gain that they want?

584
02:12:19.340 --> 02:12:23.600
Jamie (SG Eco Loop): Okay, so I now have this, question for you guys.

585
02:12:24.700 --> 02:12:25.810
Jamie (SG Eco Loop): Which is…

586
02:12:26.820 --> 02:12:35.629
Jamie (SG Eco Loop): when there is… so you want to solve a problem, and the user also has a problem. Do you think that these things are the same?

587
02:12:36.780 --> 02:12:39.960
Jamie (SG Eco Loop): Yes, no, maybe. So who thinks it's yes?

588
02:12:42.950 --> 02:12:46.880
Jamie (SG Eco Loop): Who thinks that the problem that you want to solve and the problem of the user is the same.

589
02:12:47.120 --> 02:12:48.150
Jamie (SG Eco Loop): Okay, nobody.

590
02:12:48.460 --> 02:12:50.079
Jamie (SG Eco Loop): Who thinks it's a no?

591
02:12:52.030 --> 02:12:57.619
Jamie (SG Eco Loop): Okay, some people say it's not a prop- it's not the same. Who says maybe, or it depends?

592
02:12:59.560 --> 02:13:02.600
Jamie (SG Eco Loop): Okay, some people say yes. Okay, so, actually.

593
02:13:03.630 --> 02:13:06.029
Jamie (SG Eco Loop): When you think about a problem you want to solve.

594
02:13:06.040 --> 02:13:26.069
Jamie (SG Eco Loop): You know, as someone who's in a sustainable hackathon, next time if you become a, you know, an entrepreneur who's tackling, who's building, like, sustainability-related problems, yeah, what are you thinking about? Yeah, thinking about helping the environment, you want to reduce waste, you want to reduce carbon emissions, you want to reduce inefficiencies, you want to create a better future for the future generation.

595
02:13:26.460 --> 02:13:28.500
Jamie (SG Eco Loop): Right? And other things.

596
02:13:28.630 --> 02:13:30.649
Jamie (SG Eco Loop): What are the problems of the user?

597
02:13:30.650 --> 02:13:53.210
Jamie (SG Eco Loop): Usually, the users are thinking about, okay, what is the manpower required to implement this? Do I have the money? I need to manage my customers, I have inefficiencies, I need something that's convenient. Okay, if I use this solution, I need to integrate it with my 10 other solutions that I'm already using. I need to build my branding so that my customers will continue coming to me. I need to think about regulations and more.

598
02:13:53.460 --> 02:13:58.730
Jamie (SG Eco Loop): So, actually, most likely, they have a bit different thinking to you.

599
02:13:59.270 --> 02:14:05.920
Jamie (SG Eco Loop): So what do you need to do, right? Actually, understand if this is something that is a harsh truth.

600
02:14:06.090 --> 02:14:13.829
Jamie (SG Eco Loop): for people in sustainability, you know, you and I, we're all here because we care about the environment. But the reality is.

601
02:14:14.320 --> 02:14:23.419
Jamie (SG Eco Loop): Sustainability is often not the top priority for people and businesses, especially when you talk about SMEs in Singapore, you know? They face so much trouble, they have

602
02:14:23.420 --> 02:14:39.870
Jamie (SG Eco Loop): Rental is very expensive, manpower is expensive, the cost of their ingredients, everything is rocketing, there are new, competitors coming in. So, is sustainability really the top priority for them? Most of the time, it's not.

603
02:14:40.210 --> 02:14:59.369
Jamie (SG Eco Loop): So what can you do, right, to make sure that what you are building also speaks to these people, who maybe they will help if it's easy, maybe they will help if it's not going to take away too much attention from every other problem they have, is to actually tailor your pitch to the language that they care about.

604
02:14:59.620 --> 02:15:10.970
Jamie (SG Eco Loop): So, you care about reducing waste, but what do they care about? So, I give you an example, right? Over here, I have an example. So, instead of telling them, I have this solution that will reduce waste.

605
02:15:11.100 --> 02:15:15.379
Jamie (SG Eco Loop): I will reduce 10KG of the food waste that you're throwing away every day.

606
02:15:15.660 --> 02:15:22.600
Jamie (SG Eco Loop): Instead, you can frame it in the, kind of context that matters to them, by telling them.

607
02:15:22.790 --> 02:15:36.920
Jamie (SG Eco Loop): My solution can help you reduce how much money you are wasting. Because every day you throw away this 100K of ingredients, it's costing you, like, maybe $500. With my solution, you don't have to throw them away, you save $500 every day.

608
02:15:37.220 --> 02:15:52.600
Jamie (SG Eco Loop): Now they will listen to you, correct? Because, oh, okay, I care about saving money, or I care about bringing more customers. If you use this solution, you can actually tap on the growing market of sustainability-conscious young consumers.

609
02:15:54.010 --> 02:15:57.240
Jamie (SG Eco Loop): And you will have a 10% increase in revenue.

610
02:15:57.780 --> 02:16:00.450
Jamie (SG Eco Loop): That's also something that the businesses might care about.

611
02:16:00.600 --> 02:16:08.740
Jamie (SG Eco Loop): So, it's also important… the third tip is not every user will have the same problem. So you can say, okay, your customer is restaurants.

612
02:16:08.870 --> 02:16:27.689
Jamie (SG Eco Loop): But McDonald's, and maybe a small cafe, and maybe somebody that sells coffee, sort of similar, you know, restaurants, or Chinese restaurants, that are in the mall versus Chinese restaurants, at your neighborhood area. They are own restaurants, but they don't necessarily face the same problem.

613
02:16:28.370 --> 02:16:38.879
Jamie (SG Eco Loop): So, you speak to one person, maybe that gives you a bit of confidence that you are tackling a right problem, but if you speak to more people, then you get more confidence that you are… you are targeting the right problem.

614
02:16:39.200 --> 02:16:40.129
Jamie (SG Eco Loop): Alright.

615
02:16:42.120 --> 02:16:43.910
Jamie (SG Eco Loop): Okay, so…

616
02:16:45.260 --> 02:16:57.280
Jamie (SG Eco Loop): That's why it's important to really understand your user, think from their perspective. It's this age HO saying of, like, you know, stepping into somebody else's shoes. That's what this is about.

617
02:16:58.799 --> 02:17:09.299
Jamie (SG Eco Loop): So I'm gonna do a… show you a short video, it's about 6 minutes, on, how you can ask the right questions to your users.

618
02:17:10.219 --> 02:17:10.950
Jamie (SG Eco Loop): Hopefully that's.

619
02:17:10.950 --> 02:17:12.139
Audio shared by Jamie (SG Eco Loop): Interviews are…

620
02:17:12.730 --> 02:17:14.019
Jamie (SG Eco Loop): Let me go to the first screen.

621
02:17:16.540 --> 02:17:19.060
Jamie (SG Eco Loop): Okay, let me know if you can't hear us.

622
02:17:19.790 --> 02:17:21.290
Jamie (SG Eco Loop): It should be fine.

623
02:17:21.910 --> 02:17:22.580
Audio shared by Jamie (SG Eco Loop): Hard.

624
02:17:22.660 --> 02:17:37.120
Audio shared by Jamie (SG Eco Loop): But until we can learn to read people's thoughts directly from their brains, they are the single best way to learn about your customers' mindsets, values, and belief systems. Here are 5 common mistakes to avoid. Mistake number one, not building enough rapport.

625
02:17:37.120 --> 02:17:43.879
Audio shared by Jamie (SG Eco Loop): While interviews are a great way to learn a lot about your customers, we can't just cold call someone and ask.

626
02:17:47.770 --> 02:17:52.190
Audio shared by Jamie (SG Eco Loop): Hello? Hey, this is Therese, can you tell me about your mindsets, values, and belief systems?

627
02:17:53.740 --> 02:18:10.559
Audio shared by Jamie (SG Eco Loop): You have to build rapport first. This means introducing yourself, and even making a little bit of small talk. While small talk can sound absolutely dreadful to some people, I think small talk actually gets a bad rap. It can be an incredible rapport-building detective tool.

628
02:18:10.830 --> 02:18:27.679
Audio shared by Jamie (SG Eco Loop): Hi, Rachel, nice to meet you! Hey! So, I'm Therese, I'm the researcher that will be working with you, and I work with NG, so thanks so much for taking the time to meet with us. Where are you calling in from, by the way? Oh, well, normally I'd be in Milwaukee, but today I am in Chicago.

629
02:18:27.910 --> 02:18:41.049
Audio shared by Jamie (SG Eco Loop): If the interviewer hadn't asked where they were calling from, they would never have known that the person was working from somewhere unusual, or that they were helping their family move. Those two pieces of information can say a lot about what someone might care about.

630
02:18:41.360 --> 02:18:44.059
Audio shared by Jamie (SG Eco Loop): It's easy to go too far with rapport, though.

631
02:18:44.059 --> 02:19:08.130
Audio shared by Jamie (SG Eco Loop): You mentioned you took a lift here, so are you from the area, or… Yeah, I live in Seattle. Oh my gosh, I used to live in Kirkland! Really? Yeah! So, oh, Seattle's so cool, isn't it? Yeah. I guess Kirkland's kind of changed a lot, I know, over the last 5 years. Like, I'm originally from Jersey City, and like, over there, it's like, every 5 years there's a new building, and yeah, Kirkland, I guess it's a little more stable, but, you know, Seattle is pretty cool. Yeah.

632
02:19:08.129 --> 02:19:20.059
Audio shared by Jamie (SG Eco Loop): This conversation got derailed really fast, because the interviewer made it about her own lived experiences, rather than that of the interviewee. It's easy to do that when you're trying to be seen as likable, or approachable as a person.

633
02:19:20.090 --> 02:19:27.890
Audio shared by Jamie (SG Eco Loop): But don't worry so much about being liked, and focus on being an active listener instead. Mistake number two, not probing enough.

634
02:19:28.120 --> 02:19:34.019
Audio shared by Jamie (SG Eco Loop): Probing questions are questions that help gain specific, in-depth, follow-up information about a topic.

635
02:19:34.150 --> 02:19:42.309
Audio shared by Jamie (SG Eco Loop): A lot of interviewers will shy away from probing too much, either because it feels rude to pry, or because it makes them look like they don't know much about the field.

636
02:19:42.760 --> 02:19:52.049
Audio shared by Jamie (SG Eco Loop): Yeah, the OKRs are a really high priority on the stakeholders list right now, so we've been working with PMs to get the backlog on the APIs all straightened out.

637
02:19:52.150 --> 02:19:59.180
Audio shared by Jamie (SG Eco Loop): However, playing dumb is often a good tactic to get more information that otherwise wouldn't have been offered up freely.

638
02:19:59.520 --> 02:20:07.700
Audio shared by Jamie (SG Eco Loop): Even if you have a good speculation for what some words or phrases may mean, sometimes assumptions are incorrect, so it's worth asking anyway.

639
02:20:07.700 --> 02:20:19.780
Audio shared by Jamie (SG Eco Loop): When you say PMs, that means… totally the project manager. Oh, actually, the product manager. Yeah, we use PJM for the project manager, but not everyone uses the right acronym.

640
02:20:19.950 --> 02:20:26.249
Audio shared by Jamie (SG Eco Loop): Can you tell me more about that? Oh, yeah, the wrong people reply to emails all the time. It's really annoying.

641
02:20:26.630 --> 02:20:31.319
Audio shared by Jamie (SG Eco Loop): See? Probing can shed light on problems that you may not have known existed.

642
02:20:31.460 --> 02:20:32.920
Audio shared by Jamie (SG Eco Loop): Mistake number 3.

643
02:20:33.070 --> 02:20:35.379
Audio shared by Jamie (SG Eco Loop): Multitasking during the interview.

644
02:20:35.490 --> 02:20:49.590
Audio shared by Jamie (SG Eco Loop): I get it. The texts, the pings, the calendar reminders… we don't love when these disrupt an interview, and some of it is unavoidable, even when we mute our devices. But the biggest culprit of multitasking during interviews? Note-taking.

645
02:20:49.840 --> 02:21:00.249
Audio shared by Jamie (SG Eco Loop): Not only are we terrible note-takers when we're facilitating an interview, but it also makes us poor facilitators by taking our eye contact away from the participant. Instead.

646
02:21:00.520 --> 02:21:07.160
Audio shared by Jamie (SG Eco Loop): Delegate note-taking to someone else, and when possible, record your interviews for transcription and analysis later.

647
02:21:07.470 --> 02:21:11.839
Audio shared by Jamie (SG Eco Loop): Mistake number 4. Allowing observers to influence the interview.

648
02:21:12.310 --> 02:21:30.829
Audio shared by Jamie (SG Eco Loop): An interview is a one-on-one conversation. And when people are interested in observing research, that can be a good thing. While it's not a deal-breaker to have an observer or two, imagine five people staring at you as you respond. It gets a little scary. And if more than one tries to interject at the same time.

649
02:21:31.420 --> 02:21:43.490
Audio shared by Jamie (SG Eco Loop): Yikes. If you record the interview, that can help avoid some of the issues with too many observers. But nonetheless, you should share some clear ground rules, like limiting the number of staff in the room to 3 people max.

650
02:21:43.530 --> 02:21:55.180
Audio shared by Jamie (SG Eco Loop): And have only the facilitator ask questions. After all, it's the facilitator who's built the rapport. Last but not least, the most compromising of all mistakes. Number 5.

651
02:21:55.230 --> 02:22:13.119
Audio shared by Jamie (SG Eco Loop): Leading the participant. Sometimes, researchers accidentally show their cards and say a little too much. Or they phrase a question a certain way, which primes the participant to respond differently, their answers skewed by researcher behavior. For example, if I were to ask.

652
02:22:13.120 --> 02:22:25.780
Audio shared by Jamie (SG Eco Loop): How do mobile devices play a role during in-store price comparisons? This might prime the participant to give me examples of times they've used their mobile devices to compare prices. But if I were to ask, how do you shop for appliances?

653
02:22:25.870 --> 02:22:36.419
Audio shared by Jamie (SG Eco Loop): We might find very different user behavior, like foregoing mobile devices, or maybe even uncovering new behavior, like researching brand reputations in advance. So…

654
02:22:36.490 --> 02:22:51.510
Audio shared by Jamie (SG Eco Loop): Keep the study's purpose relatively vague, keep questions as open-ended as possible, and mind your body language as you go. So there you have it! If you're interested in more user interview tactics, check out our full-day course on user interviews at nnroup.com.

655
02:22:53.650 --> 02:23:07.969
Jamie (SG Eco Loop): All right, okay, so hopefully that was helpful. This will be linked in the, slides if any of you wanted to, kind of, review that video. And then next, I'm going to… go into our last, kind of.

656
02:23:08.230 --> 02:23:22.109
Jamie (SG Eco Loop): the second diamond. So, ideas and prioritization. So, just now I mentioned a reminder that, when you are understanding your user, you need to map out the pain and the gain and make sure that your solution addresses this.

657
02:23:22.280 --> 02:23:23.709
Jamie (SG Eco Loop): So what else?

658
02:23:25.060 --> 02:23:38.049
